WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.825
[spraying]

00:00:03.498 --> 00:00:05.264
[scrubbing]

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.890
Bruce Gershon: Today, we're
going to walk you through

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.843
our restoration of a pair of Ugg boots.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:21.880
The process starts by
checking the boots in.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.880
And then we go to the cleaning process,

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.360
where Danny will be doing
what we call prespotting.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.260
He's using a steam gun

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.770
to try and clean the
soles the best possible.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.440
So, the steaming of the soles

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.950
is mostly to get the grit

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.840
and little pebbles and
grime off of the soles.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.440
It's really effective

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.120
in knocking that off

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.810
that the washing process

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:57.210
or the wet-cleaning
process alone will not do.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.740
When Danny is doing the brushing,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.170
this is with a soft nylon-bristle brush

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.505
and our formula of soap,

00:01:06.505 --> 00:01:10.371
a surfactant with oil and a solvent.

00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:13.564
It will help break down
that hard-water mark,

00:01:13.564 --> 00:01:16.220
and the products that we use

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.193
will not disturb the color.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.000
After Danny finishes the prespotting,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.510
we will go right to the machine,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.630
where we have formulas set up

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.841
for this type of cleaning.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.373
Danny: OK, in they go.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.790
Bruce: These Ugg boots are probably

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.620
generally washed at around

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.923
75- or 80-degree temperature.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.770
You have to have a little
bit of warmth in there

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.212
for the surfactants,

00:01:47.212 --> 00:01:50.143
the soaps and the oils to work properly.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.144
Danny: 20 minutes later,

00:01:53.144 --> 00:01:55.660
we're ready to take them out.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:59.360
Bruce: When we take the
boots out of the machine,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.300
there is a bit of a hopping-back,

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.150
shrinking-back process that happens

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.550
in this wet cleaning.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.160
Uggs have a tendency to stretch out

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.120
when you're wearing them,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.871
and so we are trying to

00:02:11.871 --> 00:02:13.440
stretch them back out a little bit

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.565
but more shape them,

00:02:15.565 --> 00:02:17.480
and then he's taking little

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.013
terry-cloth rags that we have

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:21.850
and putting them in the toe

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.620
so that the toe will
keep a nice round shape

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.290
when they dry.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.520
When Danny is finished shaping the boot,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.310
he'll put them on the drying rack,

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.630
and they'll usually
stay on the drying rack

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:38.503
for 24 hours to 36 hours.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.240
After we get the boots off the drying rack

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.530
and they're totally dry,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.330
Missy will take them and use a 3M pad.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.540
They make several different
grades of these pads,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.500
and this one is a light-
to medium-coarseness pad,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.740
and it's perfect for what we call shaving,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.540
or brushing the sheepskin
to bring up the nap.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.705
When the suede wears,

00:03:06.705 --> 00:03:10.290
it will literally wear
the nap off of the skin,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.020
and so one of the things that we wanna do

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.514
is brush that nap back up

00:03:15.514 --> 00:03:19.202
and make it so that you
don't see where it was worn.

00:03:19.202 --> 00:03:21.010
Danny: Now we're gonna spray them up.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.381
To the spray booth.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.460
Bruce: Missy is spraying the boot

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.740
with what we call a neutral suede oil,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.443
for rejuvenating the suede.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.250
There are water-repellent properties

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.485
to the oil.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.970
And at that point,

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:44.140
we added a little dye to the oil

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.550
to help camouflage that very slight trace

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.780
of the hard-water mark

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.430
that we were trying to eliminate.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.810
After the oiling and the drying period,

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.090
generally an hour or so,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.320
we will come back and brush up the nap,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.840
and in doing this, we're inspecting

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.082
to make sure that the color is even

00:04:07.082 --> 00:04:10.130
and we've rejuvenated the suede,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:12.063
the nap, the velvety look.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.780
Sometimes in the wet-cleaning process

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.480
the fur will pick up little
teeny bits of the suede,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.780
and so she will brush that fur

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.901
to remove any of that suede lint

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:28.360
that might've gotten on the boots

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.110
in the wet-cleaning process.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.200
So, the final step is the water repellent.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.160
The water repellent
that we use on the Uggs

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.930
is designed to help protect them

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.340
from moisture and wet-type stains.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.210
It won't make the boot waterproof.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.440
That's pretty much impossible,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.850
but it will go a long way

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:54.252
to making it repel spots and stains.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.910
And then they will be inspected,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.770
and if they go through the
final inspection process,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.760
they're ready to be packaged

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.749
and shipped off to their owner.

